# PART 6: ARCHITECTURAL CONSISTENCY AND SECURITY REQUIREMENTS

Before considering the work complete, verify consistency across all layers.

### 6.1 One authorization source of truth

Ensure that:

- Firebase Auth establishes the authenticated identity.
- Custom Claims establish privileged administrator/tutor authorization where applicable.
- Firestore data provides course assignment and enrollment information.
- Cloud Functions perform privileged operations after validating all relevant conditions.
- Firestore and Storage rules enforce client access boundaries.
- Frontend role checks are used for presentation, not as the security boundary.

Do not trust localStorage, cached profiles, frontend role hints, or user-supplied role fields for authorization.

### 6.2 Course assignment consistency

Ensure every tutor-specific operation checks actual course assignment.

This applies to:

- Course content access.
- Module uploads.
- Resource management.
- Announcement management.
- Enrollment approval.
- Notifications.
- Course analytics access.
- Tutor course listing.

An unassigned tutor must never gain access through a general tutor role.

### 6.3 Enrollment consistency

Use consistent status interpretation across:

- Cloud Functions.
- Firestore rules.
- Storage rules.
- Service functions.
- Course pages.
- Student dashboards.
- Tutor portals.
- Administrator dashboards.

Only `active` and `approved` should grant learner course access.

Pending enrollment must never grant course access.

### 6.4 Error handling

- Do not silently swallow authorization failures.
- Do not report successful operations when backend operations fail.
- Avoid returning misleading success messages after partial failures.
- Use clear error messages without exposing sensitive internal details.
- Ensure loading states and disabled controls behave correctly.
- Do not create partial enrollment, resource, or submission records when a required operation fails.

### 6.5 Existing code quality

- Avoid duplicate Cloud Functions.
- Avoid duplicate enrollment services.
- Remove obsolete insecure fallbacks.
- Remove unused imports and dead code.
- Preserve established naming conventions.
- Keep functions small and maintainable.
- Use transactions or other appropriate concurrency controls for critical operations.
- Validate all callable function inputs.
- Ensure privileged functions enforce authentication and authorization independently of the frontend.

